  "severity": "major",
  "depends_on": [],
  "risk": "low",
  "manual": true,
  "finding": "A gate check that states a RULE but not a REMEDY is not a gate, it is a wall. Phase-088 failed four times in about 24 minutes on one line: 'FAIL no test asserts a failure is produced - mandatory'. The agent received that line every time - the gateout injection added on 2026-10-16 puts the verbatim FAIL into the retry context and it appears in every Dispatch log - and it shipped correct work reporting '1774 passed, 0 failed', which is TRUE and is not what the check measures. The check asks whether any test asserts that something FAILS; a suite can be entirely green and satisfy nothing there. Phase-082 spent three attempts and about 4.5 hours rediscovering the same shape for edge_*. Both needed a human-written RETRY-NOTE.md, which is a note that should not have been necessary: verify.sh already does this correctly for one check. The missing-REPORT.md branch explains itself in five lines naming the exact file and section, and phase-019 stopped failing that check immediately. Every other check emits a bare verdict. The remedy text is known and static - it is the same advice a human writes in a retry note every time.",
  "goal": "Give every FAIL in verify.sh the remedy beside it, the way the missing-REPORT.md check already does. The remedy must be specific and actionable: name the file to change, the construct to use, and the reason the obvious alternative does not satisfy the check. Implement as a lookup table in verify.sh so the text lives in one place and every check can call it; a remedy printed from a shared helper cannot drift from the check it explains. Do not weaken, relax or skip any check. Do not change any check's PASS/FAIL logic - this phase changes only what is printed after a check has already failed. Declare manual: it changes rbops/, which the bot token cannot push.",
  "accept": [
    "every FAIL emitted by verify.sh is followed by at least one indented remedy line saying what to change, and a test asserts no FAIL line is emitted bare",
    "the missing-failure-assertion FAIL prints that a suite of entirely passing tests does not satisfy it, and shows at least two forms that do - a Result/is_err form and a #[should_panic] form",
    "the edge_* FAIL prints the exact naming convention required, with an example name",
    "the test-quota FAIL prints the current count, the required count, and where the quota is defined",
    "the missing-REPORT.md check keeps its existing explanation unchanged, proving the helper does not regress the one check that already worked",
    "no PASS/FAIL decision changes as a result of this phase - asserted by running verify.sh against a phase that passes and one that fails, before and after, and comparing the verdicts",
    "remedy text lives in one place, not copy-pasted per check, so adding a check requires adding a remedy rather than remembering to",
    "the remedy is capped in length so a failing gate's output stays readable and does not push a context over its budget",
    "a test asserts each remedy is under the cap, because an unbounded remedy is how a helpful message becomes a prompt-overflow deferral",
    "REPORT.md records the two phases this would have saved (082 and 088) with their attempt counts and elapsed times, taken from the git history rather than estimated"
  ],
