=== phase-077
Finish the list surface: `sort`, `reverse`, `zip`, `enumerate`, `filter`, `reduce`
The list surface stops halfway: `map` works (with `list_map`), `pop`/`shift` were wired in phase-032, and `filter`, `reduce`, `sort`, `reverse`, `zip` and `enumerate` are all missing — `filter`/`reduce`/`list_map` sit in `tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs::KNOWN_GAPS` explicitly deferred as higher-order work needing a walker's captured scopes. inscript ships map/filter/reduce/zip/enumerate/sort/reverse as core builtins because list transformation is everyday code, not a special occasion. The firs
GOAL: Implement `sort`, `reverse`, `zip`, `enumerate` as pure builtins and `filter`, `reduce` as higher-order builtins in both engines next to `map_builtin`, then remove all six from `KNOWN_GAPS` (which is what makes the surface test cover them instead of excusing them). Dotted module spellings (`list.sort`) resolve through the existing `stdlib::resolve` path.
=== phase-098
Add lazy iteration helpers: chain, zip, enumerate, product, groupby, lru_cache
Redblue lists are values, not iterators, so every helper that is lazy in other languages is materialised here, and the ones a program actually needs are simply missing. There is no chain to join two sequences, no zip to walk two together, no enumerate to pair with an index, no product to cross two sequences, no groupby to bucket, and no memoisation at all - so a recursive function that recomputes the same subproblem has no way to avoid it. phase-077 adds sort, reverse, zip, enumerate, filter and
GOAL: Add the iteration helpers that are not list methods: chain, zip, enumerate, product, groupby, take, skip, and an lru_cache decorator usable on a function declaration. State plainly in SPEC.md which of these are lazy and which materialise, because on a list-valued language laziness has to be spelled out rather than assumed. Memoisation must be observable - a test counts calls, not just results, because a cache that never hits looks identical to one that works until the program gets slow.
=== phase-121
Give `filter` and `reduce` an answer, or refuse them by name
src/stdlib.rs:340 registers `filter` and src/stdlib.rs:341 registers `reduce` as global builtins, and src/stdlib.rs:877-878 lists both in `BUILTIN_NAMES` — but no arm of `builtin_function` matches either name. Verified at runtime this audit: `say filter([1,2,3], to (x) give back x > 1)` answers `RuntimeError: filter was called with arguments it cannot use`, and the same program through `rb compile` + `rb vm` answers the same. Every other one of the 69 names in `BUILTIN_NAMES` was called and answ
GOAL: Make `filter(xs, f)` and `reduce(xs, init, f)` work on both VMs, or — if neither is to be implemented now — remove them from `globals()` and `BUILTIN_NAMES`, drop them from TAB completion, and say in SPEC.md § list that they do not exist. Whichever way it goes, a name the language registers must never answer with a message blaming the caller's arguments.
=== phase-101
Add the text utilities that formatting and diffing need
Redblue has text.uppercase, lowercase, trim, split, join, contains, starts_with, ends_with and replace, and nothing else. There is no wrapping to a width, no unified diff, no indentation-aware dedent, no template substitution, no padding or alignment, and no word handling - so wrapping text to a column, showing what changed between two files, and generating a file from a template are all written by hand in every project that needs them. These are boring, self-contained, and have no dependency on
GOAL: Add wrapping to a width, unified diff between two texts, dedent, a small template substitution with literal-text delimiters chosen so they cannot collide with ordinary prose, and fixed-width padding and alignment. Choose one way to spell each operation and document it in SPEC.md. Diff output must be well-formed unified diff, because the point of producing it is that other tools can read it - a diff only Redblue understands is not useful.
